origin='https://menunori.actslim.chatgpt.site'
header='''<header class="header"><a class="brand" href="/" aria-label="MenuNori home"><span class="brandmark">메</span>Menu<span>Nori</span><span class="brand-ko">메뉴노리</span></a><nav aria-label="Main navigation"><a href="/">Practice</a><a href="/guides/">Travel guides</a><a href="/about/">About</a></nav><a class="language" href="/?lang=ko" aria-label="Open the Korean practice game">한국어 연습</a></header>'''
footer='''<footer><a class="brand" href="/">Menu<span>Nori</span></a><p>A small rehearsal for your next Korean adventure.</p><div><a href="/about/">About & contact</a><a href="/privacy/">Privacy</a><a href="/terms/">Terms</a></div><small>© 2026 MenuNori · Fictional menus, real practice.</small></footer>'''
guides=[
('korean-kiosk','How to order at a Korean restaurant kiosk','A step-by-step rehearsal for Korean kiosk screens, from choosing a dish to checking your basket.', '''<p class="lead">You don’t need to read every word on the screen. Start by recognizing the choices that change your order: where you will eat, which dish you want, how many portions you need, and which options you selected.</p><h2>1. Look for a language choice</h2><p>If you see English or a language symbol, use it. An English screen can still show Korean dish names or abbreviations. If there is no language option, a translation app can help you read the labels. MenuNori prepares you to understand the flow; it does not replace translation or staff assistance at the restaurant.</p><h2>2. Choose where you will eat</h2><p>A kiosk may ask you to select 매장 (dine in) or 포장 (takeaway) before showing the menu. Another may ask near checkout. Choose according to what you actually plan to do. Takeaway packaging or prices can differ at individual restaurants; read the current screen rather than assuming the practice total will apply.</p><h2>3. Select a dish, then read its options</h2><p>On our fictional menu, tapping 떡볶이 opens spice and cheese options. The menu price is ₩4,500; adding cheese costs another ₩1,000. That makes one portion ₩5,500. Selecting two portions makes ₩11,000. Real kiosks may use different layouts, required options, or portion rules.</p><p>Distinguish an included ingredient from an extra: 치즈떡볶이 is a named dish with cheese, while 치즈 추가 is an option to add cheese to another dish. Choosing both without reading carefully could add more than you intended.</p><h2>4. Review the basket before paying</h2><p>장바구니 means basket. Check each dish name and the number beside it. 수량 is quantity; 삭제 removes an item; 합계 is total. Do not use the final button as a way to see what happens: on a real kiosk it can move you into payment.</p><table><tr><th>Korean</th><th>What it tells you</th></tr><tr><td>주문</td><td>Order</td></tr><tr><td>수량</td><td>Quantity</td></tr><tr><td>추가</td><td>Add / extra</td></tr><tr><td>삭제</td><td>Remove</td></tr><tr><td>확인</td><td>Confirm / check</td></tr><tr><td>결제</td><td>Payment</td></tr></table><h2>5. Follow the restaurant’s collection instructions</h2><p>After a real payment, the machine may display an order number or print a receipt. Keep it and follow the restaurant’s instructions. Some restaurants use a pickup counter; some bring food to your table. MenuNori stops at a simulated confirmation and never asks for a card, PIN, or payment details.</p><div class="phrase"><strong>주문하는 것 좀 도와주세요.</strong><small>Please help me place an order.</small></div><h2>Try this before your trip</h2><p>Complete the first mission without opening the hint. Then look at your basket and explain why you chose 포장. You have practiced the decision that matters, even if a real machine puts that button somewhere else.</p><p class="note">For general travel context, the <a href="https://english.visitkorea.or.kr/svc/contents/contentsView.do?menuSn=903&amp;vcontsId=229195" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Korea Tourism Organization’s dining guide</a> discusses self-service and ordering machines. This article’s menu examples are fictional.</p>'''),
('dine-in-takeaway','매장 or 포장? Dine in and takeaway in Korea','Learn the two Korean buttons that decide whether your order is for here or to go.', '''<p class="lead">Two short words can change your whole order. 매장 means the shop or premises; on an ordering screen, it indicates eating there. 포장 means packaging; in this context, it means taking your order away.</p><h2>The decision comes before the food</h2><p>Imagine you want gimbap for a picnic. You find 김밥 and choose one portion. The food is right, but selecting 매장 tells the restaurant you intend to eat there. Correct the order type before you finish. MenuNori deliberately starts with dine-in selected so that you practice noticing this detail.</p><table><tr><th>Screen word</th><th>Meaning here</th><th>Say it</th></tr><tr><td>매장</td><td>Dine in / for here</td><td>mae-jang</td></tr><tr><td>포장</td><td>Takeaway / to go</td><td>po-jang</td></tr><tr><td>매장 식사</td><td>Eating at the restaurant</td><td>mae-jang sik-sa</td></tr><tr><td>포장 주문</td><td>Takeaway order</td><td>po-jang ju-mun</td></tr></table><h2>What might change after your choice?</h2><p>The restaurant may serve your food on a tray or pack it into containers. Available menu items, packaging charges, and pickup instructions can vary. Our practice shop keeps the same menu and price in both modes so you can learn the words first. It is not a promise about prices or packaging at a real restaurant.</p><h2>Short phrases are enough</h2><div class="phrase"><strong>여기서 먹고 갈게요.</strong><small>I’ll eat here.</small></div><div class="phrase"><strong>포장해 주세요.</strong><small>To go, please.</small></div><p>You can name the dish and add the phrase: 김밥 하나 포장해 주세요 means “One gimbap to go, please.” 하나 is one. You do not need a long explanation of your plans. A clear choice and a polite 주세요 are useful starting points.</p><h2>If you selected the wrong button</h2><p>Before checkout, look for an order-type setting or return to the previous screen. If you cannot change it, ask a staff member before paying. After payment, ask whether the restaurant can change the packaging; it may not always be possible.</p><div class="phrase"><strong>포장으로 바꿀 수 있어요?</strong><small>Can I change this to takeaway?</small></div><h2>Practice the same food in both modes</h2><p>In mission 1, choose 포장 and add one gimbap and one portion of dumplings. Read the order-type line in the basket before confirming. For a second attempt, leave 매장 selected and see the feedback. The food total stays the same, but the mission is incomplete because the order type is wrong.</p><p class="note">The general meaning of a word and the label on a particular machine are not the same thing. If a screen is unclear, use translation or ask staff. This rehearsal teaches a common distinction, not a universal kiosk layout.</p>'''),
('read-korean-menu','How to read a simple Korean bunsik menu','Recognize six Korean snack-shop dishes, portion numbers, and extra-option labels.', '''<p class="lead">A menu is easier to read when you separate three things: the dish name, the price, and any options. You do not need to translate an entire menu to find your chosen food.</p><h2>Six names from our practice shop</h2><p>Bunsik (분식) refers to a casual category of food that includes dishes such as gimbap and tteokbokki. Our invented restaurant uses six recognizable dish names. The descriptions below explain our practice recipes; actual ingredients and preparation vary.</p><table><tr><th>Korean</th><th>English name</th><th>Our practice dish</th></tr><tr><td>김밥</td><td>Gimbap</td><td>Rice, vegetables, egg and ham in seaweed</td></tr><tr><td>떡볶이</td><td>Tteokbokki</td><td>Rice cakes in spicy sauce</td></tr><tr><td>라면</td><td>Ramyeon</td><td>Instant noodles in spicy broth</td></tr><tr><td>어묵탕</td><td>Fish cake soup</td><td>Fish cakes in savory broth</td></tr><tr><td>군만두</td><td>Fried dumplings</td><td>A portion of crisp dumplings</td></tr><tr><td>치즈떡볶이</td><td>Cheese tteokbokki</td><td>Tteokbokki with cheese included</td></tr></table><h2>Recognize the whole name</h2><p>김밥 is not the same as 참치김밥, which adds tuna to the name. Similarly, 치즈 before 떡볶이 changes the dish you are choosing. Match the complete name rather than stopping at the first familiar part. If a name includes an ingredient you need to avoid, check with staff before ordering.</p><h2>A portion is not always one piece</h2><p>In our menu, 군만두 하나 means one menu portion of dumplings, not one individual dumpling. A real menu may specify pieces, grams, or servings. Look for 개 (pieces or count), 인분 (servings), and 수량 (quantity). The plus button usually changes how many menu units you order; it does not necessarily change the number of pieces inside one portion.</p><h2>Read prices and extras separately</h2><p>₩3,500 is three thousand five hundred Korean won. In our shop, two gimbap cost ₩7,000. An extra is priced separately: 치즈 추가 +₩1,000 adds cheese to one tteokbokki portion. If you order two of that configured item, the extra applies to each portion in this simulation.</p><p>Real screens may combine options differently. Before paying, use the basket total to check that your interpretation matches what the machine calculated.</p><h2>When you cannot read a name</h2><div class="phrase"><strong>이거 뭐예요?</strong><small>What is this?</small></div><div class="phrase"><strong>이거 하나 주세요.</strong><small>One of this, please.</small></div><p>These phrases can help when you can point to the item. A food photograph is a clue, not an ingredient list. Do not assume a dish is vegetarian, free of allergens, or non-spicy based on its appearance.</p><h2>Your rehearsal</h2><p>Find 김밥, 어묵탕, and 군만두 without using the English names. Then run mission 3 and increase 라면 to two. Check that your basket has two noodle portions and only one dumpling portion.</p>'''),
('spice-and-extras','Mild, spicy, and extra cheese: reading Korean menu options','Understand 순한맛, 보통맛, 맵기, and 추가 without confusing mild food with spice-free food.', '''<p class="lead">Choosing the dish is only half the decision. The next screen may ask about spice or extras. Read the option labels before adding the food to your basket.</p><h2>Four useful option words</h2><table><tr><th>Word</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Use in our shop</th></tr><tr><td>맵기</td><td>Spice level</td><td>Heading above the spice choices</td></tr><tr><td>순한맛</td><td>Mild flavor</td><td>Less spicy than the regular option</td></tr><tr><td>보통맛</td><td>Regular flavor</td><td>Our default spice choice</td></tr><tr><td>추가</td><td>Add / extra</td><td>An optional additional ingredient</td></tr></table><h2>Mild does not mean no heat</h2><p>Our mild tteokbokki and ramyeon still contain some spice. A restaurant’s mild option can also be hotter than you expect. A level name is relative to that restaurant and dish; there is no universal scale that guarantees your comfort.</p><div class="phrase"><strong>이거 매워요?</strong><small>Is this spicy?</small></div><div class="phrase"><strong>안 맵게 해 주세요.</strong><small>Please make it not spicy.</small></div><p>A request does not guarantee that the recipe can be changed. Some sauces or packaged broths are already prepared. If staff say it cannot be made less spicy, choose another dish instead of assuming your request removes the heat.</p><h2>An extra and a separate dish are different</h2><p>Mission 2 asks for 떡볶이 with 치즈 추가. Selecting the separate 치즈떡볶이 dish does not satisfy this exercise, even though both totals can be ₩5,500. The mission teaches you to find and use the extra option, rather than selecting a different menu entry.</p><p>In this rehearsal, the basic tteokbokki is ₩4,500 and extra cheese is ₩1,000 per portion. Cheese tteokbokki has cheese included in its listed price. Read the item name and the option line together in the basket.</p><h2>Do not turn spice labels into dietary advice</h2><p>“Not spicy” describes the heat level of our fictional recipe. It says nothing about allergens, meat, fish, or animal ingredients. Our gimbap includes egg and ham; fish cake soup contains fish; dumpling fillings vary in real restaurants. If you have an allergy or dietary requirement, verify the actual ingredients and preparation with staff.</p><h2>Check your options before confirming</h2><p>Open 떡볶이, choose 순한맛, then select 치즈 추가. Add one portion and look for both choices underneath the dish in your basket. If you used the wrong option, remove that configured item and add it again with the right choices. Mission 2 only passes when the mode, dish, quantity, spice, and extra all match.</p><p class="note">MenuNori does not provide ingredient verification or allergy guarantees. Use this game to learn labels, and rely on the real restaurant for information about the food it serves.</p>'''),
('check-your-order','Check your Korean restaurant order before paying','A practical basket checklist for dish names, quantities, extras, order type, and totals.', '''<p class="lead">The best time to catch an ordering mistake is before payment. Use five checks: where, what, how many, which options, and how much.</p><h2>Where: read the order type</h2><p>매장 means eating at the restaurant in this context; 포장 means taking the order away. Do not assume the machine remembers your previous visit or the selection you wanted. Read the current setting in the basket.</p><h2>What: match complete dish names</h2><p>떡볶이 and 치즈떡볶이 are different menu entries. An item photograph can look similar even when the name differs. In mission 4, an incorrect tteokbokki is already in the basket. You need to remove it, then add fish cake soup alongside one gimbap. Adding the correct dishes without deleting the wrong one does not finish the mission.</p><h2>How many: check each line</h2><p>수량 means quantity. Two noodle portions and one dumpling portion should appear as 라면 × 2 and 군만두 × 1. A cart badge showing 3 only tells you the overall quantity; it cannot tell you whether the three portions are the right ones.</p><h2>Which options: look underneath the name</h2><p>Check for 순한맛 (mild), 보통맛 (regular), and 치즈 추가 (extra cheese) where relevant. If the option you want is missing, go back before paying. In MenuNori you can remove a line and add the dish again. A real kiosk may also offer a direct edit button.</p><h2>How much: do one simple calculation</h2><table><tr><th>Practice order</th><th>Calculation</th><th>Total</th></tr><tr><td>1 gimbap + 1 dumpling portion</td><td>₩3,500 + ₩3,500</td><td>₩7,000</td></tr><tr><td>1 tteokbokki + cheese</td><td>₩4,500 + ₩1,000</td><td>₩5,500</td></tr><tr><td>2 ramyeon + 1 dumpling portion</td><td>2 × ₩4,000 + ₩3,500</td><td>₩11,500</td></tr></table><p>These are invented prices. At an actual restaurant, use its current menu, option prices, and displayed final total. If something is unclear, ask before confirming a payment.</p><div class="phrase"><strong>주문 확인해 주세요.</strong><small>Please check my order.</small></div><div class="phrase"><strong>이거 하나 빼 주세요.</strong><small>Please remove one of this.</small></div><h2>Do a deliberate mistake rehearsal</h2><p>Try mission 1 with the right food but the wrong order type. The result explains the mismatch and lets you keep editing. Then correct only the mode and submit again. This is a useful habit: fix the specific mistake rather than restarting a whole order unnecessarily.</p><p>For your final rehearsal, complete mission 5 without hints. Read each line, confirm the total is under ₩9,000, and then place the practice order. You have practiced a repeatable check, not memorized a particular restaurant’s screen.</p><p class="note">Nothing is purchased or sent to a restaurant here. The confirmation screen is a simulation, and no payment information is collected.</p>''')]
def page(path,title,desc,body,kind='article'):
    directory=root/path.strip('/')
    directory.mkdir(parents=True,exist_ok=True)
    canon=origin+'/'+(path.strip('/')+'/' if path else '')
    data={'@context':'https://schema.org','@type':'Article' if path.startswith('guides/') and path!='guides' else 'WebPage','headline':title,'description':desc,'inLanguage':'en','url':canon}
    text=f'''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>{html.escape(title)} · MenuNori</title><meta name="description" content="{html.escape(desc,quote=True)}"><link rel="canonical" href="{canon}"><link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/assets/favicon.svg"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/style.css"><script type="application/ld+json">{json.dumps(data)}</script></head><body>{header}<main><article class="{kind}"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">MenuNori</a> / <a href="/guides/">Travel guides</a></div><p class="eyebrow">A SMALL REHEARSAL FOR KOREA</p><h1>{title}</h1>{body}</article></main>{footer}</body></html>'''
    (directory/'index.html').write_text(text)
for i,(slug,title,desc,body) in enumerate(guides):
    related=''.join(f'<li><a href="/guides/{s}/">{tt}</a></li>' for s,tt,_,_ in guides if s!=slug)
    page('guides/'+slug,title,desc,f'<p class="updated">By Jeremy (제레미) · Updated October 2, 2026 · Fictional menu examples</p>'+body+f'<a class="primary practice-link" href="/?mission={ [1,1,3,2,4][i] }">Put it into practice</a><h2>Keep learning</h2><ul>{related}</ul>')
cards=''.join(f'<a href="/guides/{s}/"><span>0{i+1} / PRACTICAL GUIDE</span><h3>{tt}</h3><p>{d}</p></a>' for i,(s,tt,d,b) in enumerate(guides))
page('guides','Read the screen. Enjoy the meal.','Original Korean restaurant ordering guides for travelers: kiosks, menus, takeaway, spice, and checking your order.',f'<p class="lead">Small, useful explanations for the decisions you make at a Korean restaurant. Start with a guide, then rehearse it in the practice shop.</p><img class="food-photo" src="/assets/bunsik.jpg" alt="Illustrative overhead view of gimbap, tteokbokki, ramyeon, fish cake soup, dumplings and cheese tteokbokki" width="1200" height="800"><p class="photo-caption">AI-created food illustration. Actual dishes and recipes vary.</p><div class="guide-cards">{cards}</div><a class="primary practice-link" href="/">Open the practice menu</a>','guide-index')
page('about','A little practice before Korea.','About MenuNori, a free Korean restaurant ordering rehearsal created by Jeremy.', '''<p class="lead">MenuNori helps travelers rehearse the moment between looking at a Korean menu and placing an order. It is a small, independent project created by Jeremy (제레미).</p><h2>What you can practice</h2><p>Our fictional bunsik shop has six dishes and five missions. Read the menu, choose dine-in or takeaway, adjust quantities and options, and check your basket. When you finish, the result explains what matched the mission and what needs another look.</p><h2>What makes the menu fictional?</h2><p>The restaurant name, prices, recipes, and ordering screen were created for this experience. They are not copied from a real restaurant. Food artwork is AI-generated and illustrative. Korean dish names and useful phrases are learning examples, not promises about any particular restaurant’s ingredients, prices, or service.</p><h2>How the content is made</h2><p>Menu and mission text are fixed content rather than live AI responses. The English explanations and Korean examples are prepared for this version. Independent native-speaker review has not yet been completed. We do not offer pronunciation scoring, ingredient verification, or real restaurant search.</p><h2>Free to use</h2><p>You do not need an account. No real order or payment is made. The practice interface stores only your language preference and completed mission numbers in this browser. There are no ads or analytics scripts in this version. If advertising is introduced later, the privacy information will be updated before it runs.</p><h2>Contact</h2><p>Operator: Jeremy (제레미). A public contact email has not yet been added. Direct contact and a correction channel will be provided before a public advertising launch.</p><p class="note">Preparing a trip? Use MenuNori alongside current restaurant information, translation tools, and help from staff. The game is a rehearsal of decisions, not a substitute for the actual menu.</p><a class="primary practice-link" href="/">Try your first order</a>''')
page('privacy','Privacy & your practice data','What MenuNori stores in your browser, what it does not collect, and how to clear your progress.', '''<p class="updated">Version date: October 2, 2026 · Operator: Jeremy (제레미)</p><p class="lead">The practice game does not ask for an account, payment information, recordings, or personal details.</p><h2>Browser storage</h2><p>We use localStorage for two settings: <code>menunori-language</code> stores English or Korean, and <code>menunori-completed</code> stores the numbers of missions you completed. These settings remain in this browser until you clear them. Your practice basket is held in page memory and is discarded when you reload.</p><p>This app does not send your mission results or basket to a MenuNori database. Other people using the same browser profile can see its saved preferences and progress.</p><h2>Clear your preferences and progress</h2><p><button class="secondary" id="clear-data">Clear MenuNori practice data</button></p><p id="clear-status" role="status" aria-live="polite">This clears only the two MenuNori settings in this browser. You can also use your browser’s site-data settings.</p><script>document.getElementById('clear-data').onclick=()=>{try{localStorage.removeItem('menunori-language');localStorage.removeItem('menunori-completed');document.getElementById('clear-status').textContent='Your MenuNori language preference and completion history have been cleared.'}catch{document.getElementById('clear-status').textContent='Browser storage is unavailable. Use your browser settings to clear site data.'}};</script><h2>Hosting and fonts</h2><p>The hosting service processes web requests to deliver these pages and may keep technical access information under its own policies. This private preview uses ChatGPT Sites and its access controls. Fonts are requested from Google Fonts; the request can transmit technical information such as your IP address to Google. See <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google’s privacy policy</a>.</p><h2>Advertising and analytics</h2><p>This version loads no AdSense advertising code and no analytics tracking scripts. If advertising is enabled, this notice will be revised to describe the actual providers, storage and user choices. The current notice does not claim that future advertising is cookie-free.</p><h2>Links and contact</h2><p>External links take you to other websites with their own privacy practices. MenuNori’s operator is Jeremy (제레미). A public contact address is still to be added before the advertising launch.</p>''')
page('terms','Terms of use','Terms for using MenuNori as a fictional Korean restaurant ordering exercise.', '''<p class="updated">Version date: October 2, 2026 · Operator: Jeremy (제레미)</p><p class="lead">MenuNori is a free educational simulation. Using its basket or confirmation screen does not place an order or create a payment obligation.</p><h2>Practice content</h2><p>All restaurant prices and menu configurations on the practice screen are fictional. Food images are illustrative AI-generated artwork. Restaurants may use different ingredients, prices, labels, service arrangements, and ordering screens.</p><h2>Use current information when traveling</h2><p>Check actual menu details and final prices with the restaurant before placing a real order. For allergies and dietary restrictions, ask about ingredients and preparation at that restaurant. The game’s spice labels are not ingredient or allergy assurances.</p><h2>Content and corrections</h2><p>MenuNori’s interface, explanations, and practice missions are original project content. Linked resources belong to their respective publishers. We may update examples, translations, and features. Independent native-speaker verification is still pending for this first version.</p><h2>Your browser data</h2><p>Your language and completion settings are local to your browser. See the <a href="/privacy/">privacy page</a> for details and a way to clear them. No account, audio upload, or real payment is part of this service.</p><h2>Operator</h2><p>Jeremy (제레미) operates this project. A public contact address will be added before a public advertising launch.</p>''')
# Real HTML for every route, including a friendly error page.
